  20: {title: 'Social Life and Urbanity in Safavid Iran', date: 'Tue 11/10', guiding: ['What were the major institutions of urban life in the early modern period?', 'How did these institutions help individuals cultivate new understandings of space, time, and society?'],
    ids: ['coffee'],
    beats: [
      {h: 'Sufi orders after the Mongols', p: [['🏪', 'Why Sufi orders spread (Green): lots of choices, franchise-like institutions, holy lineages back to the Prophet, local languages.'], ['🧍', 'Most people experience Islam through holy men and their shrines, and Sufi leadership becomes hereditary.'], ['✋', 'Moin: for most people sacred authority was something you could see and touch, not something in books.']]},
      {h: 'Four kinds of authority', p: [['❓', 'By 1500 there are several kinds of authority. The Safavids ask: what if one person held all of them?']],
        visual: {type: 'tree', root: 'Who has authority?', children: [{n: 'Sultan', s: 'The court, chosen by God'}, {n: 'Scholar', s: 'The madrasa, a degree'}, {n: 'Sufi master', s: 'The khanaqah, appointed'}, {n: 'Imam / mahdi', s: 'All of the above, descended from Ali'}]}},
      {h: 'The "millennial moment"', p: [['🌌', 'People everywhere expect the world to be renewed (millenarianism) and a savior to end injustice (messianism).'], ['👑', 'Rulers claim every role at once: mahdi (Shi\'i), renewer (Sunni), spiritual axis (Sufi), divinely chosen king (Persian).'], ['🪐', 'The Great Conjunction of Jupiter and Saturn: some astrologers said Islam would fade 960 years after it began (1582/83).']]},
      {h: 'Rise of the Safavids', img: 'Ismail I', map: {pins: [['Ardabil', 'Ardabil 1301'], ['Isfahan', 'Isfahan']]}, p: [['🧕', 'They start as a Sufi order in Ardabil (1301) and become a messianic movement (1447).'], ['🟥', 'The Qizilbash ("red heads," for their red caps) are Anatolian Turkmen who serve as the order\'s army.'], ['👑', '1501: Shah Isma\'il founds the empire. Followers see him as Ali reborn. His poems say "I am the shah calling you."']],
        visual: {type: 'timeline', events: [{y: 1301, t: 'Sufi order'}, {y: 1447, t: 'Messianic'}, {y: 1501, t: 'Empire'}, {y: 1588, t: 'Shah Abbas'}, {y: 1650, t: 'Strolling guide'}]}},
      {h: 'Why Ali?', p: [['🗡️', 'First imam (Shi\'i), first saint (Sufi), and warrior-hero (kings).'], ['🔗', 'Claiming Ali\'s essence meant claiming every kind of authority at once.']]},
      {h: 'From messiah to institutions', img: 'Abbas the Great', p: [['📚', 'Iran slowly becomes Shi\'i: scholars brought from Lebanon and Bahrain, new rituals, firmer sect lines.'], ['🎭', 'Shah Abbas (r. 1588–1629) deals with a rival messiah in 1593 by stepping down, crowning the rival for 3 days, executing him on day 4, and taking the crown back.'], ['🏗️', 'Reforms: capital moved to Isfahan, an army of Georgian and Circassian captives, and 150,000 Armenians moved to New Julfa to take over their trade network.']]},
      {h: 'The Guide for Strolling in Isfahan (about 1650)', img: 'Naqsh-e Jahan Square', p: [['🚶', 'The poet Rukn al-Din Semnani describes a day in the capital for friends back in Semnan, urging them to visit and build cities like it.'], ['🗺️', 'Stops: the bazaar, coffee houses, the mosque, the giant "Image of the World" square, the Si-o-se Bridge.'], ['👃', 'Themes: sights, smells and tastes, new people and products, beauty, friendship.']]},
      {h: 'Coffee houses', id: 'coffee', p: [['☕', 'Coffee: grown in Ethiopia, first drunk in Yemen in the 1400s (the port of Mokha gives us "mocha"). Sufis used it to stay awake for dhikr.'], ['💨', 'Tobacco arrives through European trade with the Americas. Hookahs are in fashion.'], ['📖', 'Coffee houses sit in arcades open to the bazaar and the square, with music and Shahnameh storytelling.']]},
      {h: 'Wine, beauty and friendship', p: [['🍷', 'Wine: drunk at court ("feasting and fighting"), with taverns run by Jews and Armenians. Rules targeted drinking in public.'], ['🎨', 'Safavid art shows beauty as gender-neutral: lovers with nearly the same face.'], ['🫂', 'The "dialogic self": you become yourself through friends, unlike Europe\'s "autonomous self." The square is huge (about 15 football fields), with clock towers and new rhythms of time.']]}
    ]},
